The fstab (file system table) file contains information about which file systems
to mount at which path at boot. It essentially works like the mount command and
takes similar parameters.

Depending on the number and types of file systems in use, the syntax be a bit
complex and difficult to read. Furthermore the amount of available options makes
a full explanation of the layout unpractical. Arch Linux includes the genfstab
script which generates an fstab file for the new system based on the mounted
file systems at the time of its execution. If all partitions and subvolumes are
properly mounted, no further modifications of the fstab file should be
necessary.

We will open the fstab file next so you can check if genfstab generated the file
correctly. As it is usually quite reliable, you do not need to worry if you do
not understand each option set. For a general understanding, see the individual
columns below. Each row represents a block device whose file system will be
mounted at boot.
    - 1. file system: identifier for the file system to be mounted; can be
        prepended by UUID= or LABEL= for alternatives to direct paths
    - 2. dir: path to the mount point of the volume
    - 3. type: the type of file system used by the volume
    - 4. options: mount options; mount uses the -o flag for setting these
    - 5. dump: whether dump (a backup utility not overly popular anymore) should
        consider this file system for backups
    - 6. pass: when a file system check is conducted by fsck at boot, this
        column determines the order in which the file systems are checked